# Three window walls holding back a pool, viewed from the dry side:
# regular GLASS (opaque-order, reference), MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS and
# STAINED_GLASS_PANE (Translucent order). The water side faces must draw
# against all three — liquid_boundary (mesh_vis_logic.hpp) keeps them out
# of the water pass and emit_boundary_films (chunk_mesh.cpp) re-emits
# them single-sided before the translucent pass, so the glass tints them
# like anything else seen through the pane and the water-side view shows
# the glass's own face, not a mirrored water quad.
# flags 9 = lighting off + fog off so the view isn't murk.
wait_idle 20 6000
# Basin x1..18 z1..8
fill 1 1 1 18 8 1 STONE
fill 1 1 8 18 8 8 STONE
fill 1 1 2 1 8 7 STONE
fill 18 1 2 18 8 7 STONE
# Pool on the far side of the test wall
fill 2 1 5 17 6 7 WATER
# Test wall at z4: three window materials, stone plug at the end
fill 2 1 4 6 6 4 GLASS
fill 7 1 4 11 6 4 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
fill 12 1 4 16 6 4 STAINED_GLASS_PANE
fill 17 1 4 17 6 4 STONE
# Dry room floor (interior x2..17 z2..3 stays flat-world air)
fill 2 1 2 17 1 3 STONE
wait 10
wait_idle 20 6000
assert_block 9 3 4 MAGENTA_STAINED_GLASS
assert_block 14 3 4 STAINED_GLASS_PANE
assert_block 4 3 4 GLASS
assert_block 9 3 5 WATER
assert_block 9 3 3 AIR
flags 9
camera 4.0 3.5 2.3 180 10
wait 30
screenshot glass_wall
camera 9.0 3.5 2.3 180 10
wait 30
screenshot stained_wall
camera 14.0 3.5 2.3 180 10
wait 30
screenshot pane_wall
# From inside the pool looking back at the dry room: the boundary film is
# single-sided, so the water side shows the glass's own face and the room
# behind it — no mirrored water quad.
camera 9.0 3.5 6.5 0 10
wait 30
screenshot stained_wall_back
camera 14.0 3.5 6.5 0 10
wait 30
screenshot pane_wall_back
echo glass_wall_water done
